<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Re-Anchoring Record ~ that it happened, never what made it valid

A guest dwells WITH a host — a dyad, one edge, and the historically fatal shape: across six surveyed hospitality institutions, one-sided admission always booked the shortfall as ''rank''. A realm's own ceremony then re-anchors that dwelling INTO the realm (`the-thing-event`).

''The mesh records THAT the re-anchoring happened. It never records what made it valid.'' Those name a DECLARATION and the grounds beneath it, and only the first belongs to this stack — with the attestor count riding as a MEASUREMENT and its sufficiency as a DECISION owned elsewhere (#/the-depth-fold).

''One artifact, folded two ways'': an EVENT on write · accrued DEPTH on read. Dwelling deposits `papa`; the depth erodes when dwelling stops. Building them separately would build them twice, because a re-anchoring attested at a position IS the datum depth accrues from.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/three-mechanisms>>

!! Three mechanisms that survive alignment ~ each shipped, none whole

''① THE UN-THRESHOLDED ATTESTOR BUNDLE.'' N independent signatures on one claim, the count carried and ''the sufficiency rule structurally absent'' — the reader decides. Two standards ship it independently:

* RFC 9162 §8.1.6 — //"It is up to a client's ''local policy'' to specify the quantity and form of evidence (SCTs, inclusion proofs, or a combination) needed to achieve compliance and how to handle noncompliance."// With §6.2 bundling SCTs from multiple logs, that reads as N attestations with no wire-format threshold.
* W3C VC Data Integrity §2.1.1 — //"A proof set is useful when the same data needs to be secured by multiple entities, but where ''the order of proofs does not matter''"//; §4.5 permits verifying //"only a subset of the proofs"//.

''And the THRESHOLD family fails by construction.'' A t-of-n signature (FROST, BLS aggregation, any threshold scheme) emits ONE signature verifying only at `t`, so the sufficiency rule bakes into the cryptography ''and the individual attestors vanish''. You cannot count what aggregated. ''Take the naive list of independent signatures''; refuse the family. The bytes buy the reader's seat.

''② THE GROUNDS-FREE BOUNDARY.'' RFC 9576 (Privacy Pass Architecture) §3.5.1 — //"The type of attestation procedure is a deployment-specific option and ''outside the scope of the issuance protocol''."// The token carries that attestation succeeded and nothing about what made it succeed; the RFC enumerates CAPTCHA, device evidence, geographic region and account validity as ''examples only''. That IS the un-modelled ruling, already normative.

''③ THE ATTESTER ⊥ MINTER SPLIT.'' RFC 9576 §4 runs four roles — Client · Attester · Issuer · Origin — and documents which collapse costs what: joint Attester-and-Issuer reads as the ''weakened'' model, split as the strong one. So:

> ''The party that runs the rite MUST NOT be the party that mints the record'', and the record MUST carry no field from which the minter could reconstruct the rite.

Enforced by ''which key signs what'', never by convention. That restates the //naming must not certify// ruling with a standards body's price attached.

''Companion:'' RFC 9334 (RATS) separates appraisal policy from evidence and gives it a ''different owner'' — Verifier Owner supplies policy, Attester supplies evidence, Relying Party supplies its own acceptance policy. ''Three policies, three owners, none reading the others'.'' The third may matter here: whether a realm honours ANOTHER realm's re-anchoring reads as the consuming realm's own policy, which the record must not presume.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-refusals>>

!! Six classes refuse, and one pattern runs under them all

|! class |! ruling broken |! the span |
| Verifiable Credentials / DIDs | global identifier per subject · status-list completeness · party revocation | a DID resolves a subject; Bitstring Status List publishes an authoritative bitmap |
| Certificate Transparency | wall clock in a merge predicate · omission made DETECTABLE | //"the log MUST fulfill this promise… within… the Maximum Merge Delay"// |
| KERI | global AID · uniqueness verdict · ''threshold set by the SUBJECT'' | duplicity concludes //"one-and-only-one valid KEL or none at all"//; TOAD rides the inception event |
| Sigstore / Rekor | CT's completeness promise + an OIDC identity binding | a global identifier per human, from a corporate IdP |
| Soulbound tokens (ERC-5192) | enumerable holder set · global identifier · linkage as a FEATURE · burn | inherits ERC-721 whole; `ownerOf` links every token one address holds |
| Ring / group signatures | the ring publishes its POOL · a group manager both admits and de-anonymises | a ring signature carries the enumerated keys of everyone who ''could have'' signed |

''The pattern:'' someone built every one of them so an outsider could ''audit the set''. This gets built so no outsider can ''constitute'' one.

''And the CT inversion reads as a gift rather than only a refusal.'' CT and `vouch-board`'s no-completeness invariant make the SAME factual observation — a log's silence stays uninterpretable — and draw opposite conclusions. CT calls it a bug and builds gossip plus MMD to kill it; this stack calls it shelter and preserves it. So ''the entire CT literature on detecting a misbehaving log reads as a threat-model catalogue'': every technique invented to make omission detectable names a technique an adversary will point at a custodian here.

''One partial survivor worth naming.'' DAA (Brickell–Camenisch–Chen) gives //"a group signature without the feature that a signature can be opened, i.e., the anonymity is not revocable"// and per-verifier ''basenames'' — linkable within one verifier, unlinkable across. That reads as ruling 4 stated cryptographically: many unlinkable faces, one per realm, and a realm still tells //the same one came back//. Its rogue-key revocation runs party-level and its TPM root reads as an empire's root; the ''basename shape'' survives the rest.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-open-tension>>

!! The plural-attestor tension ~ two cures pulling opposite ways

The dyad is the failure mode, so the record carries attestors ''PLURAL''. But RFC 9576 §6.2 documents the dual: //"increasing the number of required attestation procedures could ''decrease the overall anonymity set size''."//

Applied here: ''as the attestor count rises, the intersection of realms whose dwellers would jointly attest narrows — and the record begins naming the person by naming who vouched.'' Three attestors may identify a human more precisely than one name would.

''AND THE SAME CURE ANSWERS THE PRESENTATION LEAK, which sharpens the timing.'' A carried credential naming its group publishes that edge at every presentation, so a per-verifier pseudonym reads as load-bearing on two surfaces rather than one. ''The decision carries an expiry'': every credential issued before the basename shape lands stays correlatable for as long as it lives, so deferring the cure prices itself in credentials already in the world.

''So the dyad cure and the no-global-identifier cure pull against each other'', and no source found addresses it. The candidate shape, unresolved and operator-seated: carry attestors as ''per-realm pseudonyms'' (the DAA basename form) rather than as stable keys — a realm reads //these three dwell here// without the record naming three keys that appear elsewhere.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-record-refuses>>

!! What the type must refuse, in the type

''The omission IS the design, and a comment gets skimmed.'' A record of an unspecified ceremony reads as a strange object — it says //something happened that we may not describe// — and any later hand will feel the urge to add one validating field: a `method` string, a `rite` enum, a count. The ruling forbids it and the artifact will invite it forever.

''MUST NOT hold'': quorum rule · waiting period · franchise · test · method · rite-name · any validating predicate · any field from which a minter could reconstruct the ceremony.

''MUST hold'': the party re-anchored · the realm · a POSITION on a walkable chain (never a timestamp) · the attestors, plural, each signing independently and unordered · signatures.

''MUST NOT report'': a count as total, a set as complete, or a state as current-as-of. The read answers //does this one hold// and never //who holds//.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-depth-fold>>

!! The depth fold ~ PROPOSED: the history IS the reading, and reducing it mints what the plane forbids

The record folds two ways — an EVENT on write, accrued DEPTH on read (#/head) — and the write half stands built. Reaching for the read half turns up ''a fold no hand should write''.

`dwellingHistory` already hands back the verified, attested, chain-ordered acts: which epochs this dwelling stood re-anchored at, and who marked each. ''The obvious next move reduces that to a scalar'' — a depth, a standing, a number. ''Three standing rulings each forbid it independently'':

# ''No FROZEN rank.'' A scalar depth across dwellers IS a total order, and a total order needs a global now. A partial order — which the history already carries — needs none.
# ''Standing computed from standing drifts from merit and then locks'' (three literatures, no cure found). A depth that later gates anything becomes an input to itself.
# ''Do not render running totals'' — the count carries the concentration rather than the act, and a rendered depth invites exactly the comparison the plane exists to refuse.

And the EROSION half needs a rate nobody has seated. Two candidate shapes stand un-chosen — decay ''along the graph'' rather than the calendar, or relative standing eroding for free as others accrue (MeritRank; Koren found best accuracy at ''no decay at all'') — and picking one inside this plane would install the operator's fairness calibration as a constant.

''AND THE EROSION HALF HAS A SHAPE NOW, without a decay function.'' Make the standing a lift that ''EXPIRES rather than one that decays'': no party ever exercises a lowering act — nobody can strike a community — and a dwelling that stopped simply fails to renew. That closes the laundering path a permanent lift would open (re-anchor under a favourable reading, then let the reading change) and asks the operator for a ''renewal interval'' rather than a rate. Metrology runs exactly this as recalibration intervals.

''One more split rides here.'' `verifiedAttestors` returns a COUNT and refuses a verdict, which reads correct — and names why: the count carries a MEASUREMENT, and whether it suffices rides as a DECISION belonging to a different party. The record already enforces that by carrying no threshold; the ruling now has the vocabulary to say so.

> ''PROPOSED, not ruled:'' the depth read stands COMPLETE at `dwellingHistory`. What accrues reads as a history a party can show, never a score a system can compare. A realm that wants a threshold applies its own to the acts, in its own rite (#/unmodelled) — the same law as //the naming layer performs, so it must never certify//, one fold over.

<<~/ahu>>

